==English==

===Etymology===
Popularized by Julie Connelly in a 1989 Fortune magazine cover story, by analogy with a real estate ''trophy building.''<ref>{{cite-web|title=The CEO’s Second Wife|author=Julie Connelly|work=Fortune Magazine|date=August 28, 1989|url=http://money.cnn.com/magazines/fortune/fortune_archive/1989/08/28/72407/index.htm|passage=Powerful men are beginning to demand '''trophy wives.''' {{...}} The more money men make, the argument goes, the more self-assured they become, and the easier it is for them to think: I deserve a queen.
}}</ref><ref>{{cite-web|title=ON LANGUAGE; Trophy Wife|date=1994-05-01|work=The New York Times|author=William Safire|authorlink=William Safire|url=http://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=9F06E6DA1031F932A35756C0A962958260&sec=&spon=&pagewanted=all}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|title=The New American Dictionary of Business and Finance|entry=trophy wife|year=1990|publisher=Penguin|author=Jay M. Shafritz|author2=Daniel Oran|page=476|isbn=0451628063|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/newamericandicti0000shaf/page/476/mode/1up}}</ref> <!-- 1st edition (1983) does not include the term -->
{{rfe|en|An editor of the Wikipedia trophy wife page thinks it is older, possibly 1950 or 1965}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-trophy wife.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|trophy wives}}

# {{lb|en|derogatory}} A wife, usually [[young]] and [[attractive]], regarded as a [[status symbol]] for the [[husband]], usually [[older]] and [[affluent]].
#* {{RQ:Coupland X|chapter=It Can't Last|page=34|passage={{...}} while Mrs. Scott-Baxter, his fourth (and '''trophy) wife''', blonde and young and bored, glowered at the Baxter spawn like a mother mink in a mink farm, just waiting for a jet to strafe the facility, affording her an excuse to feign terror and eat her young.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|page=57|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/ladieshomejourna110janwye/page/57/mode/1up|title=The good, the bad and the disasters|author=Sally Ogle Davis|work=Ladies' Home Journal|month=March|year=1993|passage=But don't expect this one to break up. Arnold [Schwarzenegger] got his '''trophy wife'''—a real-life Kennedy, his entreé to the top social echelons in the country—and he's not about to let her go.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Julia Llewellyn|title=The Model Wife|publisher=Penguin UK|isbn=9780141911564
|passage=‘She's with her nanny.’ ‘Oh yeah, I forgot. You're a proper '''trophy wife''' now. Staff and everything. Well…’ She produced a bottle of cava from a plastic bag. ‘With no child to keep up appearances for, let's get  ourselves in the mood.’}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=George G. Nyman|title=Love Lost in Time Relativity|publisher=AuthorHouse|isbn=9781491834039|page=12
|passage=She was David's fourth wife. Twenty-four years his junior, they both knew why they were together. David was filthy rich, and Belinda was a '''trophy wife'''. In fact, this was her third time around in this career: Being a '''trophy wife'''.}}

